    <DataNode elementId="AT5G17050-MONOMER" textLabel="AT5G17050-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LFJ8" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="355" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The At5g17050 gene has been found to encode a flavonoid 3-O-glucosyltransferase in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; introducing a glucose group to the 3-position of the flavonoid C-ring. The gene was induced in response to plants overexpressing the PAP1 gene encoding an MYB transcription factor. Homozygote mutants bearing T-DNA insertions of the gene did not produce the protein and were found to contain significantly less anthocyanins than the wild type. The molecular function was confirmed by expressing recombinant proteins in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;. The enzyme accepted both anthocyanidins (cyanidin, pelargonidin and delphinidin) and flavonols (kaempferol, quercetin and myricetin) and converted them to the corresponding 3-O-glucosides. The enzyme also accepted flavonoids which have not been found in Arabidopsis (delphinidin, myricetin) |CITS: [15807784]|. The molecular function of At5g17050 as flavonoid/anthocyanin 3-O-glucosyltransferase was confirmed using the anthocyaninless1 (anl1) mutant of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; which showed a dramatic loss of glucosylated anthocyanin. The gene At5g17050 was expressed in all tissues investigated such as rosette leaves, stems, flower buds and roots |CITS:[17277900]|.</Comment>
